The Responses API returns citations as **annotations on the assistant’s output text**, not as a separate top-level `citations` array. With web search enabled, the response contains:

- `web_search_call`: the search operation, including its `id`, `status`, and `action`.
- `message.content[].text`: generated answer text.
- `message.content[].annotations[]`: citation metadata.
- Each `url_citation` contains `url`, `title`, `start_index`, and `end_index`, locating the citation in the text.

`response.output_text` is only a convenience string; inspect `response.output` for citation metadata. The [official web-search guide](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-web-search) also documents optionally requesting all consulted URLs via `include: ["web_search_call.action.sources"]`.

```js
import OpenAI from "openai";

const client = new OpenAI();

const response = await client.responses.create({
  model: "gpt-5.6",
  tools: [{ type: "web_search" }],
  input: "What was a positive news story from today?"
});

const citations = response.output
  .filter(x => x.type === "message")
  .flatMap(x => x.content ?? [])
  .filter(x => x.type === "output_text")
  .flatMap(x => x.annotations ?? [])
  .filter(x => x.type === "url_citation");

console.log(response.output_text);
console.log(citations.map(({ title, url, start_index, end_index }) =>
  ({ title, url, start_index, end_index })
));
```

Render these URLs as visible, clickable links, as required by the [official documentation](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-web-search).

