OpenAI returns citations as **annotations on the assistant’s text**, not as a separate top-level citations array ([official guide](https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-web-search)):

- `response.output` contains ordered items, including a `web_search_call` with its `id`, `status`, and `action`.
- The assistant’s `message.content` contains an `output_text` part with `text` and `annotations`.
- Each web citation has `type: "url_citation"`, plus `url`, `title`, `start_index`, and `end_index` identifying its location in the text ([schema](https://developers.openai.com/api/reference/resources/responses)).
- `response.output_text` is only a convenience text string; inspect `output` to preserve citation metadata.
- To retrieve every consulted URL, add `include: ["web_search_call.action.sources"]`.

```js
import OpenAI from "openai";

const client = new OpenAI();
const response = await client.responses.create({
  model: "gpt-5.6",
  tools: [{ type: "web_search" }],
  include: ["web_search_call.action.sources"],
  input: "What was a positive news story from today?"
});

const message = response.output.find(x => x.type === "message");
const text = message?.content.find(x => x.type === "output_text");

console.log(text?.text);

for (const c of text?.annotations ?? []) {
  if (c.type === "url_citation")
    console.log(c.title, c.url, c.start_index, c.end_index);
}
```

Render the returned URLs as visible, clickable links in the UI.
